{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"bc196f653e7882d2","name":"card_event_z_special_unit","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/ANZAC/events/card_event_z_special_unit","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"Z SPECIAL UNIT","text":"Deal 4 damage to a unit. If played from left-most in hand, your HQ gains +5 defense.","flavorText":"Z Special Unit was a joint Allied special forces unit formed in 1942 to operate behind Japanese lines in Southeast Asia. Although most were Australian, it also included British, Dutch, New Zealander, Timorese, and Indonesian members. Its missions included reconnaissance, sabotage, raids, intelligence gathering, and guerrilla operations, often in areas controlled by Japanese forces. Teams were inserted by submarine, parachute, or small landing craft, and the unit carried out a total of 81 covert operations in the South West Pacific during its service.Training was spread across secret sites in Australia, including Refuge Bay near Sydney, Fraser Island, Garden Island, and the Z Experimental Station near Cairns. Men learned small boat handling, demolition, close combat, parachuting, jungle warfare, and how to survive with limited support. The unit’s best known success was Operation Jaywick in 1943, when 14 commandos reached Singapore Harbour aboard MV Krait, a captured Japanese fishing vessel, and used folding kayaks to place limpet mines on Japanese ships. The raid sank or badly damaged several of them. A later raid, Operation Rimau, ended in disaster, with all 23 commandos killed or executed after capture. Z Special Unit was disbanded after the war, but its methods influenced later Australian special forces.","faction":"Anzac","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":3,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_z_special_unit","title":{"Namespace":"card_anzac","Key":"card_event_z_special_unit_title","SourceString":"Z SPECIAL UNIT","LocalizedString":"Z SPECIAL UNIT"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Anzac","kredits":3,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_anzac","Key":"card_event_z_special_unit_text","SourceString":"Deal 4 damage to a unit. If played from left-most in hand, your HQ gains +5 defense.","LocalizedString":"Deal 4 damage to a unit. If played from left-most in hand, your HQ gains +5 defense."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_anzac","Key":"card_event_z_special_unit_history","SourceString":"Z Special Unit was a joint Allied special forces unit formed in 1942 to operate behind Japanese lines in Southeast Asia. Although most were Australian, it also included British, Dutch, New Zealander, Timorese, and Indonesian members. Its missions included reconnaissance, sabotage, raids, intelligence gathering, and guerrilla operations, often in areas controlled by Japanese forces. Teams were inserted by submarine, parachute, or small landing craft, and the unit carried out a total of 81 covert operations in the South West Pacific during its service.Training was spread across secret sites in Australia, including Refuge Bay near Sydney, Fraser Island, Garden Island, and the Z Experimental Station near Cairns. Men learned small boat handling, demolition, close combat, parachuting, jungle warfare, and how to survive with limited support. The unit’s best known success was Operation Jaywick in 1943, when 14 commandos reached Singapore Harbour aboard MV Krait, a captured Japanese fishing vessel, and used folding kayaks to place limpet mines on Japanese ships. The raid sank or badly damaged several of them. A later raid, Operation Rimau, ended in disaster, with all 23 commandos killed or executed after capture. Z Special Unit was disbanded after the war, but its methods influenced later Australian special forces.","LocalizedString":"Z Special Unit was a joint Allied special forces unit formed in 1942 to operate behind Japanese lines in Southeast Asia. Although most were Australian, it also included British, Dutch, New Zealander, Timorese, and Indonesian members. Its missions included reconnaissance, sabotage, raids, intelligence gathering, and guerrilla operations, often in areas controlled by Japanese forces. Teams were inserted by submarine, parachute, or small landing craft, and the unit carried out a total of 81 covert operations in the South West Pacific during its service.Training was spread across secret sites in Australia, including Refuge Bay near Sydney, Fraser Island, Garden Island, and the Z Experimental Station near Cairns. Men learned small boat handling, demolition, close combat, parachuting, jungle warfare, and how to survive with limited support. The unit’s best known success was Operation Jaywick in 1943, when 14 commandos reached Singapore Harbour aboard MV Krait, a captured Japanese fishing vessel, and used folding kayaks to place limpet mines on Japanese ships. The raid sank or badly damaged several of them. A later raid, Operation Rimau, ended in disaster, with all 23 commandos killed or executed after capture. Z Special Unit was disbanded after the war, but its methods influenced later Australian special forces."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::OceaniaStorm","isInPermanentPool":true,"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Organic_War_Drums_4'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Organic_War_Drums_4.0"},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/Z_SPECIAL_UNIT.Z_SPECIAL_UNIT","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_anzac","Key":"card_event_z_special_unit_photo","SourceString":"Members of the 2/2nd Commando Squadron, Z Special Unit, New Guinea, 1944.","LocalizedString":"Members of the 2/2nd Commando Squadron, Z Special Unit, New Guinea, 1944."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""}},"variables":[],"functions":[{"name":"CanPlay